    <id>
        <value>lifestyle_medicine_activity.v0</value>
    </id>
    <name>
        <value>Lifestyle Medicine Physical Activity</value>
    </name>
    <description>
        <original_author>
            <item>
                <key>name</key>
                <value>Ricardo Lourenco dos Santos</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>organisation</key>
                <value>Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto (FMUP)</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>email</key>
                <value>ricardolourencosantos@gmail.com</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>date</key>
                <value>2026-01-29</value>
            </item>
        </original_author>
        <lifecycle_state>in_development</lifecycle_state>
        <details>
            <purpose>To record physical activity data from consumer wearable devices including daily step counts, active minutes, exercise sessions, and energy expenditure for lifestyle medicine assessment against WHO guidelines.</purpose>
            <keywords>physical activity, steps, exercise, active minutes, energy expenditure, METs, wearable, fitness tracker, WHO guidelines</keywords>
            <use>Use for recording:
- Daily step counts and distance
- Active minutes (moderate and vigorous intensity)
- Exercise sessions (type, duration, intensity)
- Energy expenditure (active calories, total calories, METs)
- Movement summaries and patterns
- WHO physical activity guidelines tracking (150-300 min/week moderate)

Primary use cases:
1. Daily activity summary recording
2. Exercise session documentation
3. Weekly/monthly activity trends
4. Progress toward WHO physical activity targets
5. Sedentary behavior monitoring

Compatible devices: Apple Watch, Fitbit, Garmin, Samsung Health, Google Fit</use>
            <misuse>Not for:
- Clinical exercise stress testing (requires medical supervision)
- VO2max assessment requiring ergospirometry
- Sports performance analytics requiring specialized equipment</misuse>
            <copyright>CC BY-SA 4.0</copyright>
        </details>
        <other_details>
            <item>
                <key>licence</key>
                <value>Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>custodian_organisation</key>
                <value>FMUP - Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>references</key>
                <value>1. WHO Guidelines on Physical Activity and Sedentary Behaviour. Geneva: WHO; 2020.
2. Bull FC, et al. World Health Organization 2020 guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviour. Br J Sports Med. 2020;54(24):1451-1462.
3. Tudor-Locke C, et al. How many steps/day are enough? For adults. Int J Behav Nutr Phys Act. 2011;8:79.
4. Lee IM, et al. Association of Step Volume and Intensity With All-Cause Mortality. JAMA Intern Med. 2019;179(8):1105-1112.</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>FHIR_mapping</key>
                <value>Maps to PhysicalActivitySummary profile in iOS Lifestyle Medicine FHIR IG. LOINC codes: Steps (41950-7), Distance (55430-3)</value>
            </item>
        </other_details>
    </description>
